1. See how the source is currently defined

dotnet nuget list source

If you see a Telerik entry, update it. If not, add it.

2. Add or update the source with your credentials

Add:

dotnet nuget add source https://nuget.telerik.com/v3/index.json --name TelerikFeed --username "your-telerik-email" --password "your-telerik-password" --store-password-in-clear-text

Update (if it already exists, use its name from step 1):

dotnet nuget update source TelerikFeed --username "your-telerik-email" --password "your-telerik-password" --store-password-in-clear-text

The --store-password-in-clear-text flag is needed because the dotnet CLI can’t use Windows encryption reliably. The password goes into %AppData%\NuGet\NuGet.Config for your user only, so don’t put it in a nuget.config that you commit to source control.

Better option: Telerik now supports NuGet API keys. Generate one in your Telerik account (Downloads > NuGet Keys), then use api-key as the username and the key as the password. This avoids storing your real account password, and the key can be revoked.

3. Clear the cache and restore

dotnet nuget locals all --clear
dotnet restore --force
